const pageData = JSON.parse("{\"subject\":\"Physics\",\"title\":\"Time, reference frame, and motion\",\"eyebrow\":\"\",\"introduction\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"contentHtml\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eTime, reference frame, and motion. What are these concepts? Let's reach a compromise of definition of each of them one by one.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWhat is time? This can be in the Top 10 most frequently used term in our daily conversations. \\\"I was running out of time that I didn't finish my paper\\\", or \\\"Hurry, time is up\\\", or \\\"time passes rapidly\\\". Yet our definition of time is exteremly blurry and equivocal. What? You mean time has a blurry definition? Yes, it does. The rotating arms in your watch and your morning clock does not resolve the problem: second, minute, hour, day, week, month, year, decade, century, millennium... These are all relative and arbitrary units of time.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eLet's imagine that one day you wake up with your clock ringing yet there's no light shining through the window and casting shadows onto your room: what time is it? No sunlight, you can barely see nothing. Oh no, the Sun disappears! Darkness everywhere (my poor literary word descriptions...indeed the use of time is very literary). For our ancestors, they don't have advanced technology such as a watch, a phone that synchronizes its local time with regional time zone's, or a pendulum clock. Their concept of time is established upon a relative brightness and darkness brought by the presence of Sun. This sounds more like geographical knowledge but I assume that everyone knows that our Earth is roughly a sphere and that it has a self-spin and an orbital motion around Sun as one of the elliptic orbit's focii (no worry, we will cover this later on). Also in Ancient China, people would use the shadow of a stick to indicate time, which is named \\\"日晷 (ri gui)\\\".\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAnyway, the essence of definition of time is indeed established by motion. Without motion, time is meaningless. You know how much time has passed when referring to the change of angle of the clock's hands. But what if you're situating in a dark room or simply an empty space with no motion occuring: you're a static observer, standing there. Do you know what time is it right now? Not really. I remember that NASA once conducted a survey asking participants to live in an isolated environment with no indicator of time, and the physical state of the participants change dramatically. Yet I don't want to branch off to that single experiment. In brief, time and motion are mutually reflected and referred.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eNevertheless, we don't need to force ourselves to accept this concept or establish any thorough understanding of space-time, as exploration takes time and we should follow our own flow of thoughts on the way. At current stage, let's think as Galileo or Newton, which is pretty sufficient for Newtonian mechanics.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eSo, in Newtonian mechanics, time is an absolute presence of our universe's certain property that it's something regardless of your choice of reference frame (we'll explain this concept in a few lines). A simpler but ideally equivalent statement (or premise, if you prefer) is that everyone at any position in our universe share the same time. There's another Chinese poem stating that \\\"海上生明月，天涯共此时\\\" expressing this absolute synchronizing. Yet the poets refer to a lack of geographical knowledge at that time (that he wasn't aware that our Earth is a spherical object and that it rotates); Newton's reference to absolute time is beyond the time indicated by motion of celestial body. In another word, time can be separated from space, in Newtonian mechanics (this is very important as we will reach theory of relativity in later chapters).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eOkay, after the definition of time, let's move on to reference frame. Reference frame is an extension of our previous comparison used in measurement. No reference frame, no motion, this might be one condensive summary of Newtonian mechanics's basis. Different reference frame may result in different measurement of the state of motion of the measured object. Then what is a reference frame? As you're standing on the subway station measuring the velocity of the coming subway, you're choosing yourself or the static subway station as the reference frame; when the person in the subway is measuring your relative velocity, he or she is setting itself as the reference frame. \\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAnd this concept of relative motion is from Galilean transformation: the relative velocity of one object using another object as the reference frame is the relative velocity of the measured object subtracting the relative velocity of the reference object (both measured from the third object). The Galilean transformation satisfies the addition and subtraction of vectors. What is a vector? We'll talk about it in next section. What is relative motion? A good example is that whenever you're taking a train or a taxi, another car aside goes backward, and it can be either your car is moving forward or that car is moving backward, or both are correct. Therefore, the measured velocity of one object by your reference frame suggests that in that measured/observed object's reference frame, your veolicty is the same as the one you measured. In this section, we're building up and reinforcing the concept of relativity of motion; in later section, we'll more take a closer look at a more specific definition of motion associated with vectors. See you soon.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\",\"children\":[],\"parentTitle\":\"Kinematics\",\"parentUrl\":\"/physics/interesting-topics/classical-mechanics/kinematics/\"}");
